{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said.</b> The word 'saying' is the will — as one says, 'what does your soul say,' that is, what do you wish and desire; or the sense of ויאמר is decree, in the manner of what is said (Job 22:28): ״ותגזר אומר ויקם לך״." "<b>Let there be light, and there was light.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): there were two lights. The first is the light used in this world, namely the light of the sun and the moon; and the second is the light stored away for the righteous in the world to come. And it is known that the light used in this world is emanated from the light stored away for the righteous in the world to come. And this is what the Sages, blessed be their memory, interpreted: 'the light created on high was from the wheel of the sun.' These two lights, the stored and the revealed, are alluded to in a single verse, as it is written (Psalms 31:20): ״מה רב טובך״ and so forth — 'how abundant is Your good,' for ״רב טוב הצפון״ — 'abundant is the good that is hidden' — is the stored light; ״פעלת לחוסים בך״ — 'which You have wrought for those who take refuge in You' — is the revealed light; that is, from it You wrought a revealed light for those who take refuge in You, in Your shade, in this world — and this is ״נגד בני אדם״. And do not wonder and say, 'But were not the luminaries not created until the fourth day?' — for the matter on the fourth day was that He decreed they should shine below upon the earth; this is what it says, ״להאיר על הארץ״ — 'to give light upon the earth' — for on the first day the light did not reach below, but only at the firmament." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'Let there be light, and there was light' — a mortal who lights a lamp lights it from a burning lamp; can one light a lamp from within the darkness? But the Holy One, blessed be He, brings light out from within the darkness, as it says: ״וחשך על פני תהום״, and immediately after it is written ״ויאמר אלהים יהי אור״. And thus it is written (Job 12:22): ״מגלה עמוקות מני חושך ויוצא לאור צלמות״. And Daniel said (Daniel 2:22): ״הוא גלא עמיקתא ומסתרתא ידע מה בחשוכא ונהורא עמיה שרא״ — עמיקתא, this is the matter of the Chariot; ומסתרתא, this is the matter of Creation; ידע מה בחשוכא, this is ״וחשך על פני תהום״; ונהורא עמיה שרא, this is ״ויאמר אלהים יהי אור״. And once it has been explained that He brought forth light from within darkness, we learn from this that the night includes the day, and it is possible that the night be called by the name 'day' — and from this we say on the nights of Rosh Chodesh: 'the day of this Rosh Chodesh,' and likewise on the nights of Festivals: 'the day of this Festival of Matzot,' 'the day of this Festival of Sukkot,' because the night is included within the day; but the day is not called by the name 'night,' but only by the name 'day' alone. R. Simon said: 'Five times אור is written here, corresponding to the five books of the Torah.' יהי אור — corresponds to the book of Genesis, in which the Holy One, blessed be He, engaged and created His world. ויהי אור — corresponds to Exodus, in which Israel were redeemed from Egypt and emerged from darkness, as it is written therein (Exodus 10:23): ״ולכל בני ישראל היה אור במושבותם״. וירא אלהים את האור — corresponds to the book of Leviticus, which is full of many laws. ויבדל אלהים בין האור — corresponds to the book of Numbers, in which Israel were distinguished between those who went out of Egypt and those who entered the Land. ויקרא אלהים לאור — corresponds to Deuteronomy, which is full of many commandments. In this Midrashic interpretation, in these five lights: the first is the light of the creation of the world; the second is the light of redemption; the third is the light of repentance, for one who brings an offering returns in repentance and confesses over it; the fourth is the light of the Divine Presence (שכינה); the fifth is the light of Torah and commandments." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel):</b> 'Let there be light, and there was light.' This light is the beginning of generated existence, and it is called the World of Angels, and from it came into being all the other levels beneath it, namely the heavens and the earth. And on account of the greatness of its rank, the dictum in the Chapters of Rabbi Eliezer came: 'The heavens — from where were they created? From the light of His garment.' This explains to you that the existence of the heavens derives from the truth of this light. And it says 'the light of His garment' because this light intervenes between Him and the rest of His creations, just as a garment intervenes between the one who wears it and what is outside him. And know that the Holy One, blessed be He, performs all His actions within the totality of existence by means of an angel, and each angel according to its mission. If so, the intent of this dictum — 'the heavens, from where were they created? From the light of His garment' — is that the heavens, which possess matter, were created through the power of this light, which is the World of Angels, which is form alone. And the heavens, which are matter, require the angels, which are form, for form brings about the existence of matter, and matter does not bring about the existence of form — for there can be form without matter, but there cannot be matter without form. And so this dictum is complete and does not incline toward the doctrine of the eternal pre-existence of the universe at all." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'Let there be light, and there was light' — the beginning of the coming-into-being of this light is not in this utterance, for it already was in the utterance of בראשית, which is the first utterance. And from this the Sages, blessed be their memory, said: 'בראשית is also an utterance.' And in that utterance the light was concealed, and now in the utterance יהי אור He revealed it and delimited it for contemplation therein. And this is what the masters of truth alluded to us: יהי אור ויהי אור — ויהי כן is not written here, but rather ויהי אור — it already was in the act of creation." "<b>And you should know</b> that this light is called 'good,' and it is called 'the abundant good stored away' that Israel merits; and this is what is written (Psalms 31:20): ״מה רב טובך אשר צפנת״ — 'How great is Your good that You have stored,' etc., and it is written (Isaiah 63:7): ״ורב טוב לבית ישראל״ — 'and abundant good to the house of Israel.' And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: King David, upon him be peace, said: He illumined us with that light, as it is said (Psalms 118:27): ״אל ה' ויאר לנו״, and it is written (Psalms 27:1): ״ה' אורי וישעי״ — understand this. And you need to know that now, with this utterance 'Let there be light,' the creation of the world began, and it was created on the twenty-fifth of Elul — a remembrance of the thing 'Let there be light (יהי אור),' that is, the word יהי. And what they said 'In Tishrei the world was created' — in its light, in Tishrei the creation was completed, for in it was man created, who is the completion of creation and the finish of everything, for he is the seal of the plan. And because of this they could not say 'In Elul the world was created,' for they followed the completion and the sealing." "<b>And you should gain insight</b> that the time of the precedence of Torah to the world is from בראשית until יהי אור, as hinted in the word בראשית — 'two thousand (ב׳ אלפים) years' — that it was ראשית ('first'). And thus we find in Midrash Bereshit Rabbah: ״יהי ערב״ is not written here, but rather ״ויהי ערב״ — from here we learn that there was an order of times beforehand; end of quotation. And even though time was created and before the creation there was no time, He mentioned 'times' for those two thousand years, for those days are not like the days of man, but a day of those days from which the years are without number, in the manner of what is written (Job 36:26): ״הן אל שגיא ולא נדע מספר שניו״ — 'Behold, El is great, and we do not know the number of His years' — and it is written (Job 10:5): ״הכימי אנוש ימיך״ — 'Are Your days like the days of man?' etc., and it is written (Psalms 102:28): ״ושנותיך לא יתמו״ — 'and Your years will not end.' And in those times it arose in thought to create a thousand generations, and nine hundred and seventy-four (974) generations were erased from that thousand generations — that is, they were erased in thought, not that they emerged into actuality, for the prior thought, which precedes everything, knew that they would be wicked if created. And therefore Torah was given to twenty-six (כ״ו) generations after the world was created, in order to complete the thousand generations; and this is (Psalms 105:8): ״דבר צוה לאלף דור״ — 'He commanded His word for a thousand generations' — ״בארו לסוף אלף דור״ — 'illuminated at the end of a thousand generations.' And these generations our Rabbis, of blessed memory, mentioned in the chapter 'אין דורשין': they said, 'The river of fire flows forth,' etc. 'From where does it draw? From the sweat of the living creatures. To where does it pour? Upon the heads of the wicked in Gehenna, as it is said (Jeremiah 23:19): ״הנה סערת ה' חמה יצאה״ — \"Behold, the storm of HaShem, fury, has gone forth,\"' etc. Rabbi Acha bar Yaakov said (Job 22:16): ״אשר קמטו ולא עת״ — 'who were snatched away before their time.' Said Rav Shmuel Ḥasida: these are the nine hundred and seventy-four generations who were designated to be created before the world was created and were not created; the Holy One, blessed be He, implanted them in every single generation, and they are the brazen-faced ones of the generation. End of the passage in Ḥagigah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Source cites איוב כב נח; the quotation is Job 22:28 — printed citation apparently corrupt; normalized.", 2 "ב\"ה in 'הד' אור ב\"ה' resolved as שכינה based on the parallel context of the five lights; alternatively it may abbreviate 'ברוך הוא' or similar.", 3 "הקדמות read as referring to the philosophical doctrine of primordial/eternal matter (הקדמות החומר), i.e., the Aristotelian thesis of the eternity of the universe."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:24:25.872701622Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}